WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.773
What transfers across providers, and what doesn’t.

2
00:00:03.053 --> 00:00:08.536
You will use several model families in this program, so it is worth being precise about portability.

3
00:00:08.816 --> 00:00:24.304
The fundamentals in this lesson appear, in nearly the same words, in Anthropic’s, OpenAI’s, and Google’s prompting guides: be clear and specific, give context, separate sections with delimiters, put long context first, and state the output format.

4
00:00:24.584 --> 00:00:29.811
Google adds a caution that also applies elsewhere: avoid overly persuasive language.

5
00:00:30.091 --> 00:00:45.408
Capital letters and “CRITICAL” do not substitute for a clear reason, and Anthropic’s migration notes warn that pushy instructions written to make older models act (for example “if in doubt, use the tool”) can make newer models over-trigger.

6
00:00:45.688 --> 00:00:47.203
What differs is mechanics.

7
00:00:47.483 --> 00:00:51.109
The instruction channel has different names and priority rules.

8
00:00:51.389 --> 00:00:53.971
Prefill works on some stacks and errors on others.

9
00:00:54.251 --> 00:01:04.107
Sampling parameters are rejected on the current Claude API version and ignored with a deprecation warning on recent Gemini models, according to their docs.

10
00:01:04.387 --> 00:01:07.971
Reasoning controls have different names and levels (lesson 5).

11
00:01:08.251 --> 00:01:14.544
Each provider also publishes model-specific prompting pages, because behavior shifts between versions.

12
00:01:14.824 --> 00:01:21.395
So adopt a simple rule now and keep it all program: a prompt is tuned to a model, not to “LLMs.

13
00:01:21.675 --> 00:01:28.843
” When you switch models or providers, keep the prompt, rerun the same test cases, and only then decide what to change.

14
00:01:29.123 --> 00:01:32.280
Lesson 6 builds the habit and the lab builds the tool.
